XY models are the simplest involving continuous degrees of freedom: they involve the orientation of spin vectors of unit length lying within a plane\(^{20}\). The spin orientation is described by a phase \(\theta\) that can assume any value between 0 and 2\(\pi\). Similarly, the orientation of molecules in rotator phases\(^{21}\) is a continuous degree of freedom, but there are also more subtle mappings to collective tilt distortions\(^{22}\), superconducting states\(^{23}\) and vertical shifts in periodic columnar phases\(^{24}\). If not all orientations \(\theta\) are equally likely — perhaps as a result of the local crystal field — then the system is said to be anisotropic and the crystal free energy may include so-called 'single-ion' terms taken from the series expansion \(D_{1}\mathrm{cos}\theta+D_{2}\mathrm{cos}2\theta+\ldots\). In 2D, this anisotropy is characterized by the circular harmonics. In 3D, the simplest continuous degree of freedom is the Heisenberg spin: a unit vector that can point anywhere in space\(^{25}\). The orientation of polar molecules, such as the methylammonium ions (MA\(^{+}\)) in \(\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}\) (REF.\(^{26}\)), or cation displacements, such as the off-centring of Ti atoms in \(\mathrm{BaTiO}_{3}\), can be described in this way (often with some significant anisotropy, now in 3D and, hence,
